% SLIDE 9 - Applied comment: added the implemented shoe count and penetration.
\begin{frame}{Finite-Shoe Dynamics: What Changes?}
\begin{columns}[T]
\column{0.48\textwidth}
\textbf{Infinite deck}
\[
P(C_{t+1}=c)\text{ is constant.}
\]
Cards are sampled with replacement, so the three-value observation is Markov.

\vspace{0.25cm}
\textbf{Finite environment in this code}
\begin{itemize}
    \item six decks ($312$ cards);
    \item $75\%$ penetration (cut after about $234$ dealt cards);
    \item the persistent shoe is reshuffled only before a new hand;
    \item dealer hole card stays hidden until reveal.
\end{itemize}

\column{0.48\textwidth}
\textbf{Hidden observation}
\[
s=(p,d,a)
\]
The same visible state can now imply different next-card distributions because the unobserved shoe composition changes across hands.

\vspace{0.25cm}
\begin{block}{Theoretical consequence}
For the agent, finite-hidden Blackjack is a partially observable decision process: the visible tuple aliases multiple underlying shoe states.
\end{block}
\end{columns}

\vfill
{\tiny Implementation: \texttt{src/environments/finite\_blackjack.py}.}
\end{frame}

